{"translation": "For, falling away [442] from the natural state, they have their coming-into-being in an instant of time, and from there they ascend up to the peak. For let the disease be, say, one consisting in a dyscrasia, the condition in which we have shown fever too to be constituted. It is necessary, then, that as soon as this unnatural heat first arises, the person is feverish, and that the heat is intensified up to the peak. 'For of that which has no parts, how could there be a coming-into-being in time?' These things were said by me right at the beginning as well, and I answered them by recalling the fevers that set in with chilling. But in the case of the periods the answer is sufficient; for the time of the chilling is one of the partial times of the whole disease, and the first in order. It would, however, be most ridiculous to regard the first time of the first paroxysm as the beginning of the whole disease. For this time, which they call the beginning of a disease, has its usefulness with respect to the remedies, and it was looking to this that he said: 'When diseases are beginning, if anything seems to need moving, move it; but when they are at their peak, it is better to keep quiet.'", "renderings": [{"target_no": 24, "surface": "δυσκρασίαν", "english": "dyscrasia", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 44, "surface": "θερμασία,", "english": "heat", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 81, "surface": "περιψύξεως", "english": "chilling", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 89, "surface": "περιόδοις", "english": "periods", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 107, "surface": "συμπάσης", "english": "whole", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 109, "surface": "γελοιότατον", "english": "most ridiculous", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 137, "surface": "βοηθήματα", "english": "remedies", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 156, "surface": "βέλτιόν", "english": "better", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["τῶν ἐν μέρει καιρῶν", "τὸ γάρ τοι χρήσιμον ... κέκτηται", "οὗ γὰρ ο